<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Practice</h1>
<p>Usage:
</p>
<pre> &gt; practice (skill|weapon|ability)
 &gt; practice auto
</pre>
<p>Examples:
</p>
<pre> practice strength
 practice hand axe
 practice flee
</pre>
<p>This command enables you to raise your abilities, and practice your
skills, spells and weapons. The command is used at a teacher: type
<a cmd='#'>info</a> there to see what the teacher teaches and what it costs you.
Different teachers teach different abilities and skills, spells or weapons,
and each only up to a certain percentage. From level 26 a practice also costs
money, and what it costs is different in each guild.
</p><p>When you look at your 'status' you will notice you have got a certain
amount of skill and ability practice points. The skill-points are used for
practicing spells, weapons and skills. The ability-points are used for
practicing abilities (str, dex,con, etc).
</p><p>Each time you raise a level you get more skill and ability points, and
thus you can get better abilities and better spell, weapon and
skill knowledge.
</p><p>Training costs depend on the sum of your race and profession modifiers
as follows:
</p>
<pre>  -3   19
  -2   16
  -1   13
   0   10
  +1    9
  +2    8
  +3    7
  +4    6
  +5    5
</pre>
<p>That is the cost of the first practice of something in a level. With a sum
of +1 or better you can practice the same thing a second time in that
level, for half as much again; with +3 or better more times still, each
costing half as much again as the one before. Nothing can be practised for
more than 50 points at a time. The sum also decides how far you can ever
get in it.
</p><p>Teachers won't teach you while you are under magic that changes you, such
as bless, curse, berserk or rage, or while you wear things that raise your
abilities: remove them first (see <a cmd='#'>Help Remove</a>).
</p><p>Practice auto, typed anywhere, spends your points for you with your guild's
teachers, cheapest first, and leaves the costly ones for you to choose.
</p><p><br />
